  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/assets/1cb4c2c69c93117bd5eed39322059a3c0af84d16b293ccfc053c677ac0b6127d.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA\u2248(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{\u23de}{f(2.5)+f(3)+f(3.5)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{\u0394x}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $2.5$ | $3$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $25$ | $16$ | $9$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \u2248(25+16+9+4)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =54\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{decreasing}$ over the interval $[2,4],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area.",
